      "explanation": "B.P. <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 778 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"221D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-221D\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> dipole moment\n<br><br>(I) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH : Hydrogen bonding - Hydrogen bonding &gt; Dipole-Dipole\n<br><br>(II) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl : Dipole-Dipole of C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl is more polar than C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub>\n <br>so,\nDipole-Dipole C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl &gt; Dipole-Dipole C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub>\n<br><br>(III) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> : No hydrogen\nbonding and dipole-dipole interaction\n<br><br>(IV) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub> : Dipole-Dipole\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.509ex\" height=\"1.251ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -471 667 553\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2234\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2234\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> The increasing order of boiling points will be :\n<br><br>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>\n &lt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub>\n &lt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl &lt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH\n"
